rs.\nOf course this map is functorial in <MATH_0013>, i.e., <MATH_0014>\nis a transformation of functors.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nFor sheaves in the <ACCENT_0001>tale topology we can mimic this construction, see\n<REF_0002>. To do this suppose we have\na scheme <MATH_0015>, a geometric point <MATH_0016> of <MATH_0017>, and a geometric\npoint <MATH_0018> of <MATH_0019>.\nFor any sheaf <MATH_0020> on <MATH_0021> we will construct the\n<TEXTITOPEN_0002>specialization map<TEXTITCLOSE_0002>\n<MATH_0022>\nHere we have abused language: instead of writing\n<MATH_0023> we should write <MATH_0024>\nwhere <MATH_0025> is the canonical\nmorphism. Recall that\n<MATH_0026>\nwhere the colimit is over all <ACCENT_0002>tale neighbourhoods <MATH_0027>\nof <MATH_0028>, see Section <REF_0003>. Since\n<MATH_0029> is the stalk of the\nstructure sheaf, we find for every <ACCENT_0003>tale neighbourhood\n<MATH_0030> of <MATH_0031> a canonical map\n<MATH_0032>.\nHence we get a unique factorization\n<MATH_0033>\nIf <MATH_0034> denotes the image of <MATH_0035> in <MATH_0036>, then we see that\n<MATH_0037> is an <ACCENT_0004>tale neighbourhood of <MATH_0038>.\nThis construction defines a functor from the category of <ACCENT_0005>tale neighbourhoods\nof <MATH_0039> to the category of <ACCENT_0006>tale neighbourhoods of\n<MATH_0040>. Thus we may define the map\n<MATH_0041>\nby sending the equivalence class of\n<MATH_0042> where <MATH_0043>\nto the equivalence class of <MATH_0044>.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\nLet <MATH_0045>. With <MATH_0046> and <MATH_0047>\nas above we have the <TEXTITOPEN_0003>specialization map<TEXTITCLOSE_0003>\n<MATHSEG_0001>in<MATHSEG_0002>\nNamely, if <MATH_0048> is represented by the complex <MATH_0049>\nof abelian sheaves, then we simply that the map\n<MATH_0050>\nwhich is termwise given by the specialization maps for sheaves\nconstructed above. This is independent of the choice of complex\nrepresenting <MATH_0051> by the exactness of the stalk functors (i.e.,\ntaking stalks of complexes is well defined on the derived category).\n\n<STRUCT_0006><STRUCT_0007>\nClearly the construction is functorial in the sheaf <MATH_0052> on\n<MATH_0053>. If we think of the stalk functors as morphisms of topoi\n<MATH_0054>, then\nwe may think of <MATH_0055> as a <MATH_0056>-morphism\n<MATH_0057>\nof topoi.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:825c7db29e7e359840dc14486fcdca6851aefd94da43311ff6e1fa34a52851da", "unit_id": "tag:0GJ2:prose-0001"}
